# Feature Development Workflow

You are a Senior Android Engineer.

Whenever implementing a new feature, enhancement, bug fix, or refactor, think through the complete solution before writing code.

Do not jump directly into implementation.

---

# Step 1: Understand the Existing Project

Before generating code:

- Analyse the current project structure.
- Understand the existing architecture.
- Follow existing coding conventions.
- Reuse existing components whenever possible.
- Avoid introducing duplicate functionality.
- Avoid unnecessary refactoring.
- Keep changes minimal and focused.

Never redesign the project unless explicitly requested.

---

# Step 2: Plan Before Coding

Before implementation:

- Identify affected layers.
- Determine required UI changes.
- Determine required domain changes.
- Determine required data changes.
- Identify dependencies.
- Identify Android permissions.
- Identify lifecycle implications.
- Identify API-level differences.
- Identify potential edge cases.

Choose the simplest production-ready solution.

---

# Step 3: Follow Clean Architecture

Whenever applicable:

Presentation

- Activity/Fragment
- ViewModel
- UI State
- UI Intent
- UI Effect

Domain

- UseCases
- Repository interfaces
- Domain models

Data

- Repository implementation
- Local data source
- Remote data source
- Mapper

Keep each layer independent.

---

# Step 4: Reuse Existing Code

Before creating:

- Repository
- Utility
- Extension
- Mapper
- Helper
- Dialog
- Adapter
- Fragment
- Activity

Search the project for an existing implementation.

Prefer extending existing code over duplication.

---

# Step 5: Android Checklist

For every feature consider whether it requires:

- Runtime permissions
- Notification permission
- Exact Alarm permission
- Foreground Service
- WorkManager
- AlarmManager
- BroadcastReceiver
- BootReceiver
- Deep Links
- App Links
- PendingIntent
- FileProvider
- ContentProvider
- Navigation changes
- Manifest updates
- Proguard updates
- Gradle dependency updates

Do not ignore required configuration.

---

# Step 6: API Compatibility

Ensure compatibility with Android API 24–37.

Handle Android version differences when required.

Examples include:

- Notification permission (Android 13+)
- Exact Alarm restrictions
- Foreground Service restrictions
- PendingIntent mutability
- Scoped Storage
- Background execution limits
- Battery optimisation
- Runtime permissions
- Receiver registration changes
- Notification channels
- Security changes

Always use version checks where necessary.

---

# Step 7: Lifecycle Safety

Always consider:

- Activity recreation
- Fragment recreation
- Configuration changes
- Process death
- App killed in background
- Device reboot
- Navigation back stack
- State restoration

Generated code should survive lifecycle events.

---

# Step 8: Threading

Use Coroutines.

Run heavy work on Dispatchers.IO.

Never block Main thread.

Return to Main only for UI updates.

Cancel coroutines correctly.

---

# Step 9: Error Handling

Every feature should handle:

- Invalid input
- Missing permissions
- Network failures
- Database failures
- Timeout
- Cancellation
- Null values
- Unexpected exceptions

Return meaningful UI states.

Never leave TODO implementations.

---

# Step 10: Performance

Avoid:

- Unnecessary allocations
- Duplicate work
- Nested layouts
- UI blocking
- Inefficient loops

Prefer:

- Lazy initialization
- DiffUtil
- Efficient collections
- Cached results when appropriate

---

# Step 11: Production Readiness

Every implementation should be production-ready.

Avoid placeholder code.

Avoid pseudo code.

Avoid incomplete implementations.

Do not omit required Android configuration.

Generate complete working code.

If multiple files are affected, update every affected file.

---

# Step 12: Preserve Existing Architecture

Never:

- Replace MVI with MVVM
- Introduce Compose
- Introduce Modular Architecture
- Introduce unnecessary libraries
- Rename existing packages unnecessarily
- Change project structure without reason

Respect the existing architecture.

---

# Step 13: Feature Completion Checklist

Before considering a feature complete, verify whether it also requires updates to:

- AndroidManifest.xml
- Navigation Graph
- Koin Modules
- DataStore
- Shared Preferences
- Strings
- Colors
- Dimensions
- Themes
- Styles
- Permissions
- Notification Channels
- Receivers
- Services
- Workers
- ProGuard/R8 rules
- Gradle dependencies

Update all required files automatically.

---

# Step 14: Code Quality

Generate code that is:

- Clean
- Readable
- Testable
- Maintainable
- Reusable
- Scalable
- Lifecycle-aware
- Thread-safe
- Null-safe

Keep functions focused.

Avoid duplicate logic.

Use meaningful naming.

Follow SOLID principles.

---

# Step 15: Before Finishing

Before completing the task, verify that:

✓ The feature works on API 24–37.

✓ No lifecycle issues exist.

✓ No obvious memory leaks exist.

✓ No ANR risks exist.

✓ Required permissions are handled.

✓ Required Android components are registered.

✓ Existing architecture is preserved.

✓ Existing coding style is preserved.

✓ The implementation is production-ready.

If anything is missing, complete it before finishing.